title: Data tables
intro: A game's items, spells and enemies in tables, edited like a spreadsheet, pasted from Excel and Google Sheets, and set on enemies with no code.
project: Platformer | Balance

chapter: A table of enemies | Data > New Table
menu: Data > New Table...
prompt: Enemies
explain: panel:Tables | The Tables panel | Data, New Table makes a table of game data and shows it in the Tables panel, a spreadsheet. It starts with one column and one row.
click: tables:header:Value
explain: tables:column | The picked column | Clicking a column's heading picks it. The bar above the grid names it and says what it holds.
click: tables:column:name
key: Ctrl+A
type: Health\n
columntype: Health = whole number
click: tables:addcolumn
prompt: Damage
columntype: Damage = dice
explain: tables:header:Damage | Dice | A column holds one kind of thing: numbers, whole numbers, text, yes or no, a choice from a list, dice, a sprite, sound or prefab, or a row of another table.
cell: Row 1 > Name = Slime
cell: Slime > Health = 6
cell: Slime > Damage = 1d4
expectcell: Slime > Health = 6
expectcell: Slime > Damage = 1d4
click: tables:addrow
cell: Row 2 > Name = Bat
cell: Bat > Health = 2
cell: Bat > Damage = 1
expecttable: 2 rows, 2 columns
cell: Bat > Health = lots
expectcell: Bat > Health = 2
explain: tables:message | What fits | Typing a word into a number column is refused, and the panel says what the column takes.
expectfile: Tables/Enemies.table
code: Assets/Tables/Enemies.table | Enemies.table
say: Every change is saved straight away, to a file with a line for each row, so changing a cell changes one line in git, and undo takes any change back.
hidecode

chapter: Spreadsheets | Copy and paste, both ways
say: Rows can come straight from a spreadsheet. Here are two more enemies, copied from Google Sheets.
clipboard: Ogre\t20\t2d6\nWisp\t3\t1d2
click: tables:addrow
click: tables:cell:Row 3 > Name
key: Ctrl+V
expecttable: 4 rows, 2 columns
expectcell: Ogre > Health = 20
expectcell: Wisp > Damage = 1d2
explain: tables:grid | Pasted | Control V pastes the block at the picked cell, adding rows past the bottom, named by what was pasted into the names.
click: tables:header:Health
click: tables:sortdown
explain: tables:grid | Sorted | Sorting by a column puts the strongest first. Rows and columns move, fill down and filter too.
click: tables:cell:Ogre > Name
key: Ctrl+A
key: Ctrl+C
expectclipboard: Name\tHealth\tDamage\nOgre\t20\t2d6
say: Copying every row takes the headings along, ready to paste back into a sheet. Import and export CSV, in the Data menu, do the same with files.

chapter: No code | Table Values
select: Slime
add: TableValues
pick: TableValues.Table = Enemies
set: TableValues.Sets = Health.Max = Health\nDamageOnTouch.Amount = Damage
explain: component:TableValues | Table Values | Table Values gives the slime's other components their values from a row. Each line of Sets names a property and the column it takes. With Row empty, it takes the row named like the entity.
explain: field:Health.Max | From the table | The fields it sets carry a table mark, and their tooltip says where the value comes from.
play
wait: 1
expectvalue: Slime.Health.Max = 6
cell: Slime > Health = 12
expectvalue: Slime.Health.Max = 12
explain: panel:Tables | While it plays | A change made while the game runs reaches it as its next step begins, so balancing is quick to try. Renaming a row keeps its old name working.
stop
say: Scripts read the same tables, by row and column or into a class of their own, and agents edit them through the same panel and files.

chapter: A list from one cell | Table Values
click: tables:addcolumn
prompt: Waves
columntype: Waves = text
cell: Slime > Waves = Bats, Slimes, Ogre
select: Slime
add: GameSpeed
set: TableValues.Sets = Health.Max = Health\nDamageOnTouch.Amount = Damage\nGameSpeed.Speeds = Waves
explain: field:GameSpeed.Speeds | A list from a cell | A property holding a list of names takes a text cell split on commas and line breaks, trimmed, with empty pieces dropped. The field carries the table mark like the others, and a script's list of names works the same.
